+  "entries": [
+    {
+      "id": "M3",
+      "knowledge": "Appending a general non-desklamp procedure to the existing desklamp-only skill—while also narrowing the desklamp procedure to tasks that explicitly require a desklamp—was examined on 10 paired ordinary put/clean/transfer tasks whose scenes contained no desklamp. Because no desklamp appeared, the scope-boundary clause was never triggered and its effect is unobserved; the operative change was the appended guidance (open closed containers upon reaching them and inspect before leaving; avoid immediately revisiting an already-open empty receptacle; avoid repeated empty-handed inventory checks; act only on the exact requested target rather than a substitute; clean only when the task requires it; then go directly to the destination, opening it if closed, and place). Under these conditions the Parent skill succeeded on 6 of 10 tasks and hit the 50-step limit on 4, while the Candidate succeeded on 7 and hit the limit on 3. The clearest contrast is the paired clean-a-dishsponge-and-put-it-in-shelf task: the Parent cycled among the sinkbasin, cart, shelf 1 and shelf 2 without ever inspecting the bathtubbasin, eventually picked up a soapbottle (a non-target substitute) and moved it to shelf 1 without completing the task (step limit); the Candidate explored additional receptacles, found the exact dishsponge at the bathtubbasin, took it, cleaned it at the sinkbasin, and placed it on shelf 1 for a success. This supports that added search breadth combined with exact-target/no-substitute guidance can rescue a task on which the Parent substituted the wrong object, though the two clauses act together and cannot be isolated. The Candidate also opened closed containers earlier than the Parent in the toiletpaper and knife tasks (e.g., opening drawers and finding the toiletpaper inside drawer 2, or opening the destination drawer before fetching the knife), consistent with the 'open closed containers on reaching' clause; this added inspection steps in otherwise easy tasks (8 vs 4 steps, 7 vs 4, 10 vs 9) but did not prevent success. In simple tasks where the target was immediately reachable and cleaning was required (clean apple into diningtable, clean lettuce into fridge, clean plate into fridge), Candidate and Parent behavior was step-for-step identical (acquire, clean at sinkbasin, open the closed destination, place), and put-only tasks involved no cleaning detour, so the direct-completion and clean-only-if-required clauses did not degrade these cases. However, when the requested object was absent from every inspected receptacle, both Parent and Candidate fell into the same failure mode: in the dishsponge-on-shelf, kettle, and lettuce-to-diningtable tasks both reached the step limit, with the Candidate still shuttling repeatedly among already-inspected receptacles (though in the dishsponge task it opened more distinct cabinets than the Parent). Thus the 'do not immediately revisit an already-open empty receptacle' clause was not reliably followed and did not by itself break the cycling/step-limit failure mode. Limitations: one stochastic trial per task, the success improvement is concentrated in a single paired task, and the desklamp-scope narrowing could not be evaluated because no desklamp task was run.",
