+      "knowledge": "The actual diff also rewrote the search step to add: use current and recent observations to avoid immediate cycling; do not re-examine an already-open empty receptacle or check inventory while carrying nothing; and, if the obvious cabinets/fridge have not yielded the target or the step count is already high, prefer uninspected drawers, shelves, the diningtable, or other listed receptacles and new unopened containers over returning to known-empty locations. The observed response is mixed. Positive case: in one cool-a-bread instance the Parent cycled among the fridge, cabinets and countertops and hit the step limit without finding bread, whereas the Candidate expanded to the diningtable (reached during search), took a bread there, cooled it at the fridge, and placed it on countertop 1, succeeding. Negative case: in a same-task pair with an identical room and identical container contents, the Parent explored countertop 3 and took the bread there, while the Candidate repeatedly visited countertop 2 but never went to countertop 3, cycled among cabinets, shelves and the fridge instead, and hit the step limit. In the cool-a-plate pair the Candidate repeatedly chose an unavailable move to cabinet 3 (falling back to a no-op) and then examined that already-empty location several times, reached the plate in cabinet 9 only at step 49, and exhausted the budget one step later while the Parent completed the same task at step 38 after finding the plate in cabinet 9. In the remaining unsolved tasks (cool a pan, heat a mug) both Parent and Candidate shuttled for many steps among the same cabinets, stoveburners and other receptacles instead of exhausting uninspected locations. Overall, the Candidate showed occasional broader exploration consistent with the edit (the diningtable find) but the anti-cycling/step-budget-priority language was not reliably followed, cycling and step-limit failures persisted, and the net task-level result across the ten pairs was slightly lower for the Candidate (6 successes vs 7), which is within plausible stochastic variation and cannot be attributed to this clause alone; each task was run once and location of the target varied across instances.",
